  "theorem_name": "SignedSubhorizonSurvivorSignatureIndistinguishability",
  "declared_proposition": "Fix an even N having both a Goldbach representation and a bad odd candidate pair. Let tau_N be the largest bad-pair least-factor gate, let rho_N be the largest smaller endpoint of a prime pair, and put sigma_N=min(tau_N,rho_N). For every integer Y<sigma_N, one prime pair and a bad pair have the same truncated small-factor survivor signature through depth Y. Consequently no function of that signature alone, linear or nonlinear and with arbitrary signed weights, classifies every candidate pair correctly.",
  "mathematical_argument": "Choose a bad pair attaining tau_N and a prime pair attaining rho_N. Through every depth below sigma_N, neither witness is removed by a trial divisor, so both signatures are all ones. Equal inputs receive equal outputs under every deterministic function; therefore signed depth weights do not repair the information loss.",
